  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nConvection is ongoing north of the Missouri River following the \nintensification of a now 30-40 kt low-level jet against the \nstationary boundary draped across portions of northern MO and \neastern KS. Recent mesoanalysis depicts 2500-3000 J/kg MLCAPE across \nthe CWA with a gradient of PWATs between 2.0 and 2.5 from the I-70 \ncorridor up through the IA/MO border, respectively. Storms clusters \nare already showing the tendency to congeal and train in an \nenvironment lacking adequate deep-layer shear. Additional \ndevelopment is expected as the low-level jet continues to provide \nlift in the frontal zone overnight, increasing potential for \ntraining roughly along and north of US-36. A severe threat does \nstill exist, albeit marginal, with isolated strong wind gusts \npossible, but flash flooding remains the main concern as we progress \nthrough the overnight with continued training and weak upper-level \nflow. Backbuilding will cease as the low-level jet decays later this \nmorning. Weakening showers and storms may linger briefly after \nsunrise before coming to an end mid/late morning.\n\nThe stationary boundary will hold over northern MO/southern IA for \nmuch of today as the ridge begins to slide east. Hot and humid \nconditions are once again expected with heat products in effect \nuntil late this evening for areas generally in the vicinity of US-36 \nand south. Any lingering cloud cover could limit temperatures today, \nespecially across northern MO. Max heat indices could sit around \n105F south of the river with heat indices around 100F further north, \nbut this forecast will be sensitive to the persistence of cloud \ncover.\n\nThe stationary front is expected to sag south late Saturday night \ninto early Sunday morning, bringing storm chances to the region once \nagain. Northern MO once again appears to have the greatest \nlikelihood of storms (50-70% chance), though there is a 20-30% \nchance into far east-central KS and central MO. There are questions \nregarding the cold front behind the stationary front that is set to \nmove through the area sometime between early Sunday morning and late \nSunday night. Some guidance projects the passage to occur by \nsunrise, leading to cooler conditions (high temperatures in the \nlow/mid 80s) areawide, while others suggest a slower progression \nthat leads to the temperature gradient dividing the CWA (low 80s in \nthe north versus low 90s in the south). Needless to say, there is \nconsiderable timing uncertainty having significant implications for \nSunday's temperature forecast.",
